  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "The passage first gives a generalized example in which children recognize one another and their kin, making it impossible to conceal a sister; after repayment to those who raised her, she returns to her brother, and this is called a 'case of the navel.' It then introduces a historical family episode said to resemble this pattern: during the desolation and scattering of the country, Umagushu married Umanjonga, but she left on the way back to her own people, was lost from him in the general confusion, and later was known to have gone to another place.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "In the generalized example, children know one another and the names of their people.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Those who raised the sister cannot conceal her; they ask for repayment, receive it, and the sister returns to her brother.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "This matter is explicitly called a 'case of the navel.'",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The narrator says there is another matter resembling the navel case, but it has become indistinct because of the desolation of the country.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "During a time of scattering after conflict with the Dutch, the narrator's family had two paternal uncles younger than their father; the youngest was Umagushu, who had married Umanjonga shortly before the dispersal.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Umanjonga left on the way and returned to her own people.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The edition's note explains that the description of Umanjonga as already 'beautiful' is a euphemism meaning that she was pregnant.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Umagushu continually searched for his wife but could not find her because people were moving in confusion.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The narrator's group and Umanjonga eventually arrived in different places, and the family kept speaking about her.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    }
