    "notes": "Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."
  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "This appendix discusses monsters in and around the Zulu tale corpus. It identifies Isikgukgumadevu as a major Zulu river monster able to live on land, notes a many-headed monster in the tale of Usikulumi, and then surveys comparable giant or monstrous beings from Basuto, classical, European, Polynesian, American Indian, Hindu, Muslim, Ojibwa, and Norse traditions.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The appendix says Zulu legends imply a former existence of giants and contain belief in monsters, but it says this belief did not arise from observing large fossil remains.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Isikgukgumadevu is identified as the great monster of these tales and is described as a river monster that can also live on land.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The passage states that Isikgukgumadevu answers to the Kammapa of the Basuto legends.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "In the tale of Usikulumi, a many-headed monster is said to be destructive in its usual habits but friendly to Usikulumi.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The author lists other monstrous beings for comparison, including Hydra, the Minotaur, a sea monster, a dragon, a three-headed loch monster, a seven-headed dragon, and a monstrous cuttle-fish.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3",
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The passage also names large or supernatural beings from other traditions, including Mishe-Nahma, Hari in fish form during a deluge, a world-supporting tortoise and elephant, a speaking camel from a cleft mountain, a mountain-like dormouse, Jormungand, Fenrir, the Nemean Lion, a great bear of the mountains, and Cadmus's serpent.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5",
        "ev:6",
        "ev:7",
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Several monsters in the catalog are described as swallowing people, ravaging lands, encircling the world, being bound, or being slain.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4",
        "ev:5",
        "ev:7",
        "ev:8"
